  "result": "The node did not pass: the gate's final verdict is `UNANCHORED_VARIABLE`. I stopped there because the problem is in the node, not something I can fix in the two files.\n\n**Why it fails:** the rule has to range over signal heads and pick the one the pedestrian faces. The only link the node claims between the pedestrian and that head is \"facing\": a comparison of `dev.position` with the pedestrian's `kin.position` and `kin.heading`. The gate does not accept that comparison as tying the head to the pedestrian. It accepts only `rel_occupies`, `st_region_geometry_distance`, `dev_governs` or a relation to the pedestrian, and the node claims none of these for the head. The rules say to report this rather than work around it with an atom the node doesn't claim.\n\n**What the plan agent must change**, in span s3 (\"facing such a signal\"), one of:\n- claim an anchoring atom for the head, such as `dev.governs` over the roadway or crosswalk the pedestrian is about to enter. That adds a condition the words don't state, so the record would need to justify it; or\n- if a comparison of the head's position with the pedestrian's position is meant to count (rule 3b mentions \"a comparison that reads both\"), change the harness's check to accept it for devices.\n\nThe sibling node NRS_484B_307_2b uses the same atoms for \"facing\" and will likely hit the same check.\n\n**Where the files stand:**\n- **Rule:** fully written in `NRS_484B_307_4b.lean`, with my formulas explained in its header.\n  - Class: pedestrian aggregate.\n  - Applicability: the pedestrian faces a head showing a green turn arrow alone, within reach and the cosine bound; it was off the roadway at the previous observation; and none of the four 283 exceptions applies.\n  - Obligation: the pedestrian is not on a roadway.\n- **Witnesses:** the five planned scenarios, plus four tests I added that all expect not applicable: already on the roadway, facing away, head out of reach, and arrow shown with a red ball.\n- **Not checked yet:** the gate stopped at the anchoring check, so it has not run the scenarios or the 283 exceptions. Those exceptions are untested against real scenes, because I couldn't read those rules.\n\n**Gate runs:** 2 of 6 used. I spent the first on the unedited skeleton by mistake. I also once tried the gate with `--help`, which the permission layer blocked; nothing ran and it didn't use a run.\n\nThe full report is in `task_result.md` in the working directory.",
